From 64b6a01a51d225ca5f9c26ffacaf2894458e1c9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: permissionBRICK <40219477+permissionBRICK@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:48:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(camera): MIPI IR sensors with raw monochrome output

Laptops with MIPI cameras behind a CSI-2 receiver without a monochrome
ISP path (e.g. Snapdragon X with Qualcomm CAMSS: an HM1092 IR sensor next
to an OV02C10) get only the IR sensor's raw format from libcamera's simple
pipeline, R10_CSI2P. Biopass rejected it, and could not tell the two
cameras apart, since both report the same /dev/video nodes.

- Accept R10_CSI2P, R10, R12_CSI2P and R12, unpack them and stretch to
  8-bit grey: black level from the 0.5th percentile (at most 1/16 of full
  scale), white from the 99.5th, with a minimum span so a dark frame stays
  dark. Such a stream is treated as IR (grey session) whatever was asked.
- Frames of an emitter that strobes on its own: dark ones (off phase) are
  no samples; a capture keeps the brightest lit frame and returns a dark
  one only when nothing lit arrives (covered lens).
- Honour libcamera's mounting orientation: request Rotate0 and turn the
  frames upright in software when the sensor cannot flip (all formats).
- A camera may be named by its libcamera id; a video node shared by
  several cameras picks the monochrome one for IR and the colour one for
  the main camera; auto-select prefers a colour camera.
- Only one camera streams at a time: the main session is closed before
  the IR session opens and the other way round. Cameras behind one
  capture pipeline cannot stream together.
- Clear errors for a camera in use by another application (-EBUSY on
  acquire) or a busy capture pipeline (-EBUSY on start).
- camera_capture_test resolves an index, id or model selector to the
  camera id, so it captures from the right one of such cameras.
