+### MIPI IR sensors (raw monochrome)
+
+Some laptops (e.g. Snapdragon X with Qualcomm CAMSS, or Intel IPU) connect the IR sensor over MIPI CSI-2 without an ISP that handles monochrome sensors. libcamera's simple pipeline then offers only the sensor's raw format, such as `R10_CSI2P` (10-bit packed, `Y10P`), `R10`, `R12_CSI2P` or `R12`. Biopass reads these as IR streams:
+
+- The values are unpacked and stretched to 8-bit grey: the darkest pixels (0.5th percentile, at most 1/16 of full scale) become black, the brightest (99.5th percentile) white. A frame with almost no light keeps a minimum stretch, so it stays dark instead of turning sensor noise into an image.
+- Dark frames, as an emitter that strobes on its own delivers in its off phase, don't count as samples: each capture keeps the brightest lit frame, and returns a dark one only when no lit frame arrives (covered lens).
+- If the camera is mounted rotated (libcamera's `Rotation` property, from the devicetree or ACPI) and the sensor cannot flip, Biopass turns the frames upright in software. This applies to every format.
+
+Such cameras often share all their `/dev/video*` nodes (one CSI-2 receiver for both sensors), so a node does not say which camera it is. Biopass then uses the monochrome sensor for the IR camera and the colour sensor for the main camera. To pick a camera explicitly, put its libcamera id (as `cam -l` or `camera_capture_test --list-devices` prints it, e.g. `/base/soc@0/cci@ac15000/i2c-bus@0/camera@24`) into `camera` or `anti_spoofing.ir_camera` in `config.yaml`; the app shows it as an unavailable device but keeps it. For the IR sensor as the main camera, set both to its id.
+
+Only one camera streams at a time: the main camera is closed before the IR camera opens, and the other way round. Cameras behind one capture pipeline cannot stream together, and USB cameras share the bus. A camera that another application holds fails the attempt at once; PAM then falls through to the next method.
+
 ## 3. If The IR Emitter Stays Off On Linux
 
 If the IR camera is detected but its emitter does not light up, use [`linux-enable-ir-emitter`](https://github.com/EmixamPP/linux-enable-ir-emitter):
